          ['card-p8-1','mod-4',8,'H-4 EAD Definition','What is the H-4 EAD and who is eligible for it?','The H-4 EAD (Employment Authorization Document) is a work permit available to certain dependents of H-1B visa holders, specifically those on H-4 visas. It allows eligible H-4 visa holders to legally work in the United States.','Visa Rules',['Employment Authorization Document','dependents of H-1B visa holders','legally work in the United States']],
          ['card-p10-1','mod-5',10,'Single Filing Status Criteria','Who qualifies for the Single (S) tax filing status?','You must file as single if you are unmarried. Single filers include people who are unmarried or legally separated from a spouse under a divorce or separate maintenance decree on the last day of the year and who do not qualify for another filing status.','Filing Status',['unmarried','legally separated','divorce or separate maintenance decree']],
          ['card-p10-2','mod-5',10,'Married Filing Jointly (MFJ) Liability Rule','On a Married Filing Jointly return, how is tax liability shared between spouses?','Both spouses are equally responsible for the return and the taxes. If either one understates the taxes due, both are equally liable for the penalties, unless the other spouse can prove they were unaware of the mistake and did not benefit from it.','Filing Status',['both spouses are equally responsible','equally liable for the penalties','unaware of the mistake']],
          ['card-p11-1','mod-5',11,'Head of Household (HOH) 3 Key Criteria','What three criteria must a married person filing separately meet to claim Head of Household (HOH) status?',"1. Your spouse did not live with you for the last six months of the year.\n2. You provided the main home of the qualifying child and paid for more than half the home costs.\n3. You are claiming your child as a dependent.",'Filing Status',['did not live with you for the last six months','paid for more than half the home costs','claiming your child as a dependent']],
          ['card-p12-1','mod-5',12,'Qualifying Widower With Dependent Child (QWDC)','What are the rules for filing as Qualifying Widower With Dependent Child (QWDC)?','You may file as a qualifying widow or widower for the next two years beginning the year after your spouse\'s death, and your dependents must stay with you for at least six months. The survivor must remain unmarried for at least two years. The taxpayer must have at least one dependent child and have handled at least half of the household costs.','Filing Status',['next two years','at least six months','remain unmarried for at least two years','half of the household costs']],
          ['card-p13-1','mod-6',13,'ITIN Definition & Structure','What is an ITIN, what is its purpose, and what number does it always start with?','ITIN stands for Individual Taxpayer Identification Number. It is a nine-digit number used to claim your dependents and spouse on your tax return. Formatted as XXX-XX-XXXX. It always begins with the number 9.','Taxpayer ID',['Individual Taxpayer Identification Number','not eligible for SSN','formatted as XXX-XX-XXXX','begins with the number 9']],
          ['card-p14-1','mod-6',14,'Criteria to Apply for ITIN','What are the US stay criteria for a spouse and child dependent to apply for an ITIN?',"• A spouse must spend at least 1 to 90 days in the United States to claim a tax year.\n• Child dependents should be in the US for at least 183 days to claim a tax year.",'ITIN Criteria',['spouse must spend at least 1 to 90 days','Child dependents should be in the US for at least 183 days']],
          ['card-p14-2','mod-6',14,'How to Apply for ITIN & Processing Time','How do you apply for an ITIN, what form is attached, and how long does it take from the IRS?',"• A passport front and back copy, a visa copy, and the applicant's basic information are required.\n• The W7 form is attached to the 1040 (federal documents).\n• TP must schedule an appointment at the nearest TAC (TAXPAYER ASSISTANCE CENTER) office.\n• To receive an ITIN, it takes at least 6 to 8 weeks from the IRS.",'ITIN Application',['passport front and back copy','W7 form attached to 1040','TAC (TAXPAYER ASSISTANCE CENTER)','takes at least 6 to 8 weeks']],
          ['card-p15-1','mod-7',15,'W-2 vs. Wage Statement & W-2c Purpose','What is the difference between a wage statement and a W-2 form, and what is Form W-2c used for?','A wage statement is a breakdown of your wages for the pay period, while a W-2 is your annual wage and tax statement. A W-2c is used to correct errors on a W-2 such as incorrect names, amounts, or Social Security numbers.','Wage Statements',['breakdown of wages for the pay period','annual wage and tax statement','correct or amend a W-2']],
          ['card-p16-1','mod-7',16,'W-4 Allowances Effect','How does increasing allowances on Form W-4 affect paycheck withholding?','Increasing the number of allowances on the form decreases the amount of money withheld from the paycheck.','Tax Withholding',['increasing the number of allowances','decreases the amount of money withheld']],
          ['card-p17-1','mod-8',17,'E-Filing Notification Timelines','How quickly does a tax filer receive an acceptance or rejection notice when E-Filing?','The tax filer receives an acceptance or rejection notice within 48 hours—usually within 24 hours—of transmitting the tax return. Acceptance is proof that the documents have been received and are in the system.','E-Filing',['within 48 hours—usually within 24 hours','Acceptance is proof','rejection alerts the taxpayer']],
          ['card-p18-1','mod-8',18,'FBAR Filing Threshold & Account Types','Who must file an FBAR and what is the combined account balance threshold?','U.S. persons (U.S. citizens, Green Card holders, resident aliens, and dual citizens) are required to file an FBAR if the combined balance of all foreign accounts is more than $10,000 at any point during the calendar year. Types: Bank Accounts, Investment Accounts, Other Financial Accounts.','FBAR Compliance',['more than $10,000 at any point during the calendar year','financial interest or signature authority','Bank Accounts, Investment Accounts']],
          ['card-p19-1','mod-9',19,'1095-A, 1095-B, and 1095-C Differences','What are the distinct purposes of Form 1095-A, Form 1095-B, and Form 1095-C?',"• 1095-A: Marketplace Statement – reconcile premium tax credits.\n• 1095-B: Health Coverage – confirm minimum essential coverage.\n• 1095-C: Employer-Provided Health Insurance – report employer mandate compliance.",'Health Insurance Forms',['1095-A Marketplace Statement','1095-B Health Coverage','1095-C Employer-Provided']],
          ['card-p20-1','mod-9',20,'1098-T Tuition Fee Document Scope','What is Form 1098-T used for and what visa restriction is stated for it in the manual?','1098 (T) - Tuition Fee Document F1 to H1b only. The 1098-T form reports tuition expenses billed at a college or university. Used to claim education credits such as the American Opportunity Credit and the Lifetime Learning Credit.','Education Forms',['F1 to H1b only','tuition expenses','American Opportunity Credit','Lifetime Learning Credit']],
          ['card-p21-1','mod-10',21,'Form 1099-DIV & 1099-NEC Thresholds','What are the reporting dollar thresholds for Form 1099-DIV and Form 1099-NEC?',"• Form 1099-DIV: Issued if you received more than $10 in dividends.\n• Form 1099-NEC: Issued for payments of $600 or more to non-employees.",'1099 Forms',['more than $10 in dividends','$600 or more to non-employees']],
          ['card-p22-1','mod-10',22,'401(k) Retirement Savings Plan Definition','How does a 401(k) Retirement Savings Plan work according to the manual?','Retirement savings plan sponsored by an employer. It lets workers save and invest a piece of their paycheck before taxes are taken out. Taxes aren\'t paid until the money is withdrawn from the account.','Retirement Plans',['sponsored by an employer','before taxes are taken out','Taxes aren\'t paid until withdrawn']],
          ['card-p22-2','mod-11',22,'Standard Deduction Amounts for Tax Year 2023','What are the exact 2023 Standard Deduction amounts for all filing statuses?',"Amounts for Tax Year 2023:\n❖ Single Filers: $13,850\n❖ Married Filing Jointly: $27,700\n❖ Head of Household: $20,800\n❖ Married Filing Separately: $13,850\n❖ Qualifying Widow(er): $27,700",'Standard Deductions',['Single Filers: $13,850','Married Filing Jointly: $27,700','Head of Household: $20,800','Married Filing Separately: $13,850','Qualifying Widow(er): $27,700']],
          ['card-p23-1','mod-11',23,'Schedule E Purpose & Common Deductions','What is Schedule E used for and what are common Schedule-E deductions?','Schedule E reports income and expenses from rental property, royalties, estates, and trusts (Form 1040 attachment). Common deductions: Mortgage Interest, Property Taxes, Insurance Premiums, Repairs and Maintenance, Depreciation, Management Fees, Advertising, Legal and Professional Fees.','Deductions',['rental property, royalties, estates, and trusts','Depreciation','Management Fees','Repairs and Maintenance']],
          ['card-p24-1','mod-12',24,'Receiving Your Tax Refund Options','What three methods can a taxpayer choose to receive their refund?',"• Direct Deposit: Directly deposited into your bank account for faster processing.\n• Check: Paper check mailed to your address.\n• Apply to Next Year: Applied as an estimated payment for the next year's taxes.",'Tax Refunds',['Direct Deposit','Check','Apply to Next Year']],
          ['card-p25-1','mod-12',25,'ITIN Credits & Child Tax Credit Rules','Can taxpayers using an ITIN claim the Child Tax Credit, and what exception exists?','A qualifying child must have a valid SSN to claim the Child Tax Credit. If a taxpayer uses an ITIN, they generally cannot claim the Child Tax Credit. Exception: The Credit for Other Dependents (up to $500 per qualifying dependent) is available to taxpayers with ITINs.','Tax Credits',['qualifying child must have a valid SSN','cannot claim the Child Tax Credit','Credit for Other Dependents (up to $500 per qualifying dependent)']],
          ['card-p25-2','mod-12',25,'Form 8879 and Form 7216 Definitions','What are Form 8879 and Form 7216 used for?',"• Form 8879: Declaration document and signature authorization for an e-filed return (ERO). E-Filing authorization document.\n• Form 7216: Authorization to file the taxes on behalf of the client.",'Tax Authorizations',['Form 8879 signature authorization for an e-filed return','Form 7216 authorization to file the taxes on behalf of the client']],
          ['card-p26-1','mod-12',26,'Who is E-Tax Planner CPA?','Who is the CPA of E-Tax Planner and what is his background?','Our CPA is MR.JOBY THOMAS who is Retired from IRS. After his retirement he started his own Firm as E- TAX Planner. We are in the Market from past 12 Years.','Firm CPA',['MR.JOBY THOMAS','Retired from IRS','past 12 Years']],
        ];

